Why Each Component Was Added

1. Load Balancer (HAProxy)
- Distributes incoming requests between two servers.
- Prevents any single server from being overwhelmed.
- If one server fails, traffic is routed to the other.

2. Two Web/Application Servers
Each server hosts:
- Nginx (web server)
- Application server
- Application files (code base)
- MySQL database instance

Why:
- Provides redundancy: if one server goes down, the other still works.
- Allows higher traffic capacity by splitting load.
- Improves reliability and performance.

3. Primary-Replica Database Setup
- Ensures data redundancy.
- Replica offloads read requests, improving performance.
- Protects data by having multiple copies.


Load Balancer Details

Distribution Algorithm Used: Round Robin

How It Works:
- The load balancer sends each new request to the next server in order.
- Example: Request 1 ->  Server 1, Request 2 ->  Server 2, Request 3 ->  Server 1, etc.
- Ensures equal traffic distribution.

Active-Active vs Active-Passive

Active-Active (Used Here)
- Both servers actively process requests at the same time.
- Load balancer splits user traffic between them.
- Better performance and uptime.

Active-Passive (Not Used Here)
- Only one server handles all traffic.
- The second server waits in standby mode.
- Takes over only if the active server fails.
- Less efficient and wastes resources.


Primary - Replica Database Cluster

How It Works:
- Primary database handles all write operations.
- Replica database receives a continuous copy of all changes via replication.
- Application writes go to the Primary; read requests can be served by the Replica.

Primary Node:
- Handles INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE.
- Source of truth for the data.

Replica Node:
- Read-only.
- Used for SELECT queries.
- Helps reduce load on the Primary.


Issues With This Infrastructure

1. Single Points of Failure (SPOF)
- The load balancer is a SPOF. If it fails, no traffic reaches any server.
- The Primary database is a SPOF. If it fails, the system cannot handle any writes.
- Application files are duplicated manually on each server -  no automatic synchronization.

2. Security Issues
- No firewall to restrict inbound/outbound traffic.
- No HTTPS, so data travels unencrypted and vulnerable to interception.
- Databases are not isolated; they are exposed to potential attacks.

3. No Monitoring
- No monitoring tools like Nagios, Zabbix, Prometheus, or Datadog.
- Cannot detect server overload, downtime, replication lag, load balancer failure, etc.
- No alerting system to notify administrators of critical issues.

